## Verify Configuration Before Boot

Before booting compute nodes, verify that boot-service and metadata-service are
configured correctly. These checks catch the most common failures before a
wasted boot cycle.

First, confirm that boot-service has the correct kernel parameters stored:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify boot-service boot config is uploaded -->
```bash
ochami -k boot config get -F yaml \
	| grep -q "ds=nocloud-net" \
	&& echo "boot-service: OK" \
	|| { echo "boot-service: FAILED - check boot config"; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Next, confirm that the cloud-init default configuration was uploaded
successfully:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify cloud-init defaults are uploaded -->
```bash
ochami -k metadata defaults get \
	| grep -q "base_url" \
	&& echo "cloud-init defaults: OK" \
	|| { echo "cloud-init defaults: FAILED"; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Next, confirm that the compute group cloud-init configuration is present.
This is what delivers the SSH keys and munge key to the node at boot:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify cloud-init compute group is uploaded -->
```bash
ochami -k metadata group list -F yaml \
	| grep -q "compute" \
	&& echo "cloud-init group: OK" \
	|| { echo "cloud-init group: FAILED"; exit 1; }
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Finally, confirm that the metadata-service resolves each compute node by its
IP address. This is the lookup the node itself performs at boot, and the only
check here that has to run after node discovery.

The server answers `/metadata-service/meta-data` from SMD's Ethernet
interfaces. In OpenCHAMI 0.2.0 the metadata-service resolves a miss live from
SMD (cache-first with a live fallback), so a node discovered a moment before it
boots should no longer get an empty answer. This probe still guards against a
server that is not ready yet: wait for the lookup to succeed before powering the
nodes on.

The request below is the one the node will send. `X-Forwarded-For` carries the
node's address so that the server resolves the same entry; haproxy appends its
own entry to the header and the server reads the first.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify the metadata-service resolves each compute node -->
```bash
for ((i=0; i<num_computes; i++)); do
	for ((t=0; ; t++)) ; do
		curl -fsS -m 5 -o /dev/null \
			-H "X-Forwarded-For: ${c_ip[$i]}" \
			"http://${sms_ip}:8081/metadata-service/meta-data" && break
		(( t < 18 )) || {
			echo "cloud-init node: FAILED for ${c_name[$i]}"
			exit 1
		}
		echo "Waiting for cloud-init to resolve ${c_name[$i]}..."
		sleep 5
	done
done
echo "cloud-init node: OK"
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->
